==English==
{{was wotd|2007|July|11}}

===Etymology===
{{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|invidiōsus}}, from {{m|la|invidia||envy, ill will}}, from {{m|la|in-||upon}} + {{m|la|videō||I see}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ɪnˈvɪdiəs/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-invidious.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɪdiəs|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|of a distinction}} [[offensive|Offensively]] or [[unfair]]ly [[discriminate|discriminating]].
#* '''1891''', [[w:Henry James|Henry James]], ''The Marriages'':
#*: "Yes, you must have a lot of places," the Colonel observed, looking at her shining raiment with a sort of '''invidious''' directness.
# {{lb|en|of an action or task}} Causing [[ill will]] towards the actor; causing [[offense]].
# {{lb|en|of a thing}} Causing [[envy]] or ill will towards the possessor.
# [[envious|Envious]], [[jealous]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[detestable|Detestable]], [[hateful]], [[odious]].

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|invidiously}}
* {{l|en|invidiousness}}

